Handling an exception
• If a code may raise an exception, better to use a try: block to 
defend
• After the try:  block, include an except statement.
• Syntax:
try:
Perform your operations here;
......................
except Exception I:
execute this block. 
except Exception II:
execute this block. 
except Exception ..
…………..
else:
If there is no exception then execute this block.

Handling an exception
•Example:
try:
fh = open(“file1.txt", "r") 
fh.write(”my name is XYZ”) 
except IOError:
print("Error: can\'t find file or read data")
else:
print("Written content in the file successfully")
fh.close()

Handling an exception
• Except clause with multiple exceptions:
•You can also use the same except statement to handle multiple 
exceptions as  follows:
try:
Perform operations here;
......................
except(Exception1[, Exception2[,...ExceptionN]]]):
If there is any exception from the given exception  list, then 
execute this block
.......................
else:
If there is no exception then execute this block.

Handling an exception
• Except clause with no exceptions can be used to catch all types of 
exceptions.
try:
You do your operations here;
......................
except:
If there is Exception, then execute this block.
......................
else:
If there is no exception then execute this block.
• The above type of exception handling is not considered a good  
programming practice. though,

Handling an exception
while True:
try:
n = int(input("Enter a number: "))
fh = open("testfile.txt", "w")
fh.write("This is my test file for exception handling!!")
sum=0
for i in range(n+1):
sum=sum+i
except IOError:
print("File Error")
break
except ValueError:
print("Invalid entry, Please retry")
else:
print("Sum is: ",sum)
break
finally:
fh.close()
